= Device driver — Simple =

<onlyinclude>
A '''device driver''' is software that helps a computer or other system operate a particular device. The operating system can offer a common way to ask for data or send commands, while the driver knows how to communicate with the device. A driver may control a physical component, such as a network adapter, or a virtual device implemented by software. It is not always a separate program you install: some drivers are built into the operating system.<ref name="linuxsimple">Linux kernel documentation, [https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/driver-model/binding.html Driver Binding], how systems associate devices and drivers.</ref>


== A concrete example ==
Imagine an application sending a file over a network. The application does not normally need to know the exact command format of every network adapter. The operating system and networking software prepare packets and request transmission. A network driver then communicates with a compatible adapter, possibly placing descriptors in a queue and notifying the device. Later the adapter or driver reports whether work completed. The exact steps depend on the hardware and operating system.

The same general idea applies to keyboards, cameras, graphics processors, storage controllers and sensors. However, different devices expose very different interfaces; not every driver moves data in the same way.

== Finding the right driver ==
A computer often identifies a device using information supplied by a bus or platform. The operating system looks for a compatible driver and attempts to connect it to the device. In Linux this is called ''binding'', and a driver may run a ''probe'' operation to initialize resources. Finding a matching identifier does not guarantee that the device is ready or functioning.<ref name="linuxsimple"/>

If no suitable driver is present, some features may be unavailable. A driver may also be present but fail because of incompatible firmware, missing dependencies, configuration problems or damaged hardware. Updating a driver can fix a bug, but the newest driver is not automatically the safest or most compatible for every device.

== Driver, firmware and operating system ==
The '''operating system''' manages broad computer resources and provides interfaces to programs. A '''driver''' provides particular device behavior within or alongside that environment. '''Firmware''' is software associated with a hardware platform or device, often running on its own controller. A '''bootloader''' prepares and starts another program, commonly an operating-system kernel. These roles can overlap in a product but are not synonyms.

For example, a network adapter can contain firmware that controls internal functions, while a driver on the host computer prepares work and interprets responses. Both may be needed, but they execute in different contexts.

== Drivers are not all equally privileged ==
Some drivers execute in the operating-system kernel, where a serious bug can affect the whole system. Others run in user mode with more limited permissions. Windows Driver Frameworks include both KMDF and UMDF approaches.<ref name="wdfsimple">Microsoft Learn, [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/wdf/using-the-framework-to-develop-a-driver Using WDF to Develop a Driver].</ref> Which is appropriate depends on the device, available operating-system facilities and security requirements.

== What can go wrong? ==
A device can be unplugged while an operation is running. A driver might receive an unexpected response, time out, exhaust memory or lose power during a transfer. Reliable drivers need to handle these situations without corrupting data or leaving the system in an unsafe state.

A driver update is not the same thing as a hardware repair. Problems should be diagnosed with device information, system logs, compatibility documentation and reproducible tests rather than by guessing that every failure is caused by the driver.


